# PermissionContext / Casbin-format policy (VCP)

Every authorization decision — in pages, components, shards, procedures,
**and** in `view!` markup — must go exclusively through a request-scoped
`PermissionContext` built from the Casbin-format policy CSV via
`PolicyStore` (`src/perms.rs`). VCP does **not** depend on the `casbin`
crate and does **not** ship a Casbin `model.conf`.

This portal is **not** the bastion. Do **not** copy `../Vauban` policy
CSV files or resource names. Tenant isolation on `/{org}/…` paths is a
separate hard gate (see `portal-security.mdc`).

## In Topcoat pages and components

Load permissions once per request from `Cx` (memoized helper). Prefer
functions over middlewares.

```rust
async fn require_perms(cx: &Cx) -> Result<PermissionContext> { /* … */ }

#[page] // /admin/docs — staff nest under /admin/*
async fn admin_docs(cx: &Cx) -> Result {
    let staff = require_staff(cx).await?; // admin_view; non-staff → 404
    let perms = perms_for_user(cx, &staff.user).await;
    if !perms.docs_write {
        return Err(capability_denied().into()); // entry deny → 404
    }
    view! { /* … */ }
}
```

- **Never** gate on `is_staff` / `is_superuser` / `is_admin` / raw role
  string compares.
- **Never** treat “logged in” as enough for org data: policy capability
  **and** membership on the path `{org}` slug (404 if not a member).
- Admin nest (`/admin/*`): layout gate via `require_staff` (`admin_view`)
  **plus** body re-check; clients/anonymous get **404** (anti-enumeration).

## Permission catalogue (mockup IA)

Map `PermissionContext` fields 1:1 to `(resource, action)`. Source of
truth: `config/access/vcp_policy.csv`.

| Resource     | Actions                         | Typical surfaces                          |
|--------------|---------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|
| `docs`       | `read`, `write`                 | KB / admin documentation editor           |
| `builds`     | `read`, `download`              | Certified builds list / download          |
| `releases`   | `manage`                        | Admin release manager                     |
| `issues`     | `read`, `write`                 | Issue tracker / report                    |
| `companies`  | `manage`                        | Admin client companies                    |
| `key`        | `manage`                        | Admin security keys (WebAuthn / KEY)      |
| `account`    | `read`                          | Account & subscription                    |
| `admin`      | `view`                          | Admin rail / nest entry                   |

Field naming: `docs_read`, `docs_write`, `builds_read`, `builds_download`,
`releases_manage`, `issues_read`, `issues_write`, `companies_manage`,
`key_manage`, `account_read`, `admin_view`.

Adding a new field MUST come with:

1. A matching `p, role:..., r, a` line in `config/access/vcp_policy.csv`.
2. A tracked-permission registry + drift test (every permission granted
   to at least one role).
3. Denial-path tests (wrong role, wrong org, anonymous).

## In `view!` templates

Actionable controls must read policy flags on `PermissionContext`:

```rust
// FORBIDDEN
if user.is_admin { /* show release manager */ }

// OK
if perms.releases_manage { /* … */ }
```

## Forbidden patterns

| Forbidden | Required instead |
|-----------|------------------|
| Role booleans as gates | `perms.<resource>_<action>` |
| Trust path `{org}` without membership | `require_org(cx, slug)` |
| Capability alone for object access | `PermissionContext` + tenant / ownership |
| Bastion `assets:*` / `sessions:*` | VCP catalogue only |
| Missing `PermissionContext` | Fail closed |
| Shipping / reading `model.conf` | CSV + `PolicyStore` only |

## Two-layer authorization

1. **Policy (`PermissionContext`)** — capability (may download builds /
   manage releases?).
2. **Tenant** — membership on this org slug; prefer **404** over chatty
   403 when cross-tenant existence would leak.

Hard invariants (e.g. max **5** user accounts per company) live in
dedicated helpers, not the policy CSV alone.

## Why

One `PermissionContext` keeps HTML and handlers aligned with policy.
Tenant checks prevent IDOR when a capability is broadly granted.
